  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and thunderstorms return and increase \nalong with increasingly more humid for the first half of next \nweek. There is also the potential for areas of heavy rain and \nsome strong to severe thunderstorms Tuesday.\n\nAs a strong upper-level ridge expands into the Plains early \nnext week, northwesterly flow aloft will occur across much of \nthe East. This ridge gets pushed back some as a potentially \nstrong upper-level trough in eastern Canada sends spokes of \nenergy southeastward across the Great Lakes and Northeast. This \nwill initially allow for the upper-level trough to become more \ncarved out across the Northeast by later this weekend. Some \nguidance maintains a vigorous shortwave riding over the ridge \nand sliding southeastward across the Great Lakes Monday and then\nacross the Northeast during Tuesday. This results in some model\nsolutions continuing to close off the trough across the \nNortheast Tuesday into Wednesday. Some other guidance is slower \nand weaker and thus does not close it off or briefly closes it \noff a day or two later. The upper-level system will drive \nsurface low pressure and a cold front our way Tuesday into \nWednesday. It will become much more humid during the first half \nof next week, with dew points looking to surge on Tuesday.\n\nThis will all result in an increase in convective chances \nMonday and especially Tuesday into Wednesday. While there \nremains some differences among the model guidance on the timing \nand strength of the features diving southeastward early next \nweek, the setup would continue to favor upstream convective \ndevelopment given northwesterly flow aloft. This would be \nenhanced if the trough aloft closes off. Deeper moisture (dew \npoints into the low 70s) advecting northward ahead of this \nfeature with eventual cooling aloft and stronger forcing for \nascent should result in widespread showers and some \nthunderstorms which maximizes on Tuesday. Depending on the \nevolution of this system, some areas of heavy rainfall \n(precipitable water values increase to 1.5-2.00 inches) and some\nsevere thunderstorms are possible. The intensity and coverage \nof thunderstorms though may partially depend on the prior days \nconvective development and then its evolution downstream.",
